/* Original independent OCR H446 SLR 4-5 student revision dataset; no assessment items. */
const SLR45_DATA={"topics":[{"id":"os-role","slr":4,"spec":"1.2.1(a)","title":"Why an operating system is needed","desc":"User interfaces, resource management and the OS as an intermediary","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr04-need-for-operating-systems/","html":"\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>operating system (OS)\u003c/strong> is system software that provides services for applications and manages interactions between the user, programmes and hardware. It abstracts complex hardware details so application developers do not have to control each device directly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"flow\">\u003cspan>User\u003c/span>\u003cb>↔\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Applications\u003c/span>\u003cb>↔\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Operating system\u003c/span>\u003cb>↔\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Hardware\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>User interface\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The OS provides ways to issue commands and receive feedback, such as \u003cb>GUI\u003c/b> (windows, icons, menus and pointer), \u003cb>CLI\u003c/b> (typed commands), menus and touch interfaces. These provide different trade-offs in ease of use, automation and resource use.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Memory management\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Allocates and protects areas of RAM for programmes, tracks which pages/segments belong to each process, and handles cases where RAM is insufficient.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Interrupt handling\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Responds to asynchronous events from hardware and software by temporarily suspending other execution and running an appropriate service routine.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Processor scheduling\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Decides which ready process uses the CPU next, aiming for useful utilisation, fairness and acceptable response times.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Other OS services\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>File management\u003c/b> names and organises files, directories and permissions. \u003cb>Device management\u003c/b> provides drivers, buffers and queues. \u003cb>Security\u003c/b> includes user accounts, privilege separation and resource protection. \u003cb>Process management\u003c/b> coordinates programme execution and access to devices.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original example: a public archive terminal\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A visitor opens a catalogue, downloads a description and sends it to a printer. The OS presents the interface, allocates memory for the browser, schedules its CPU time, handles network and printer events, and manages files without the visitor controlling printer registers directly.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Remember for evaluation questions\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Do not just list functions. Explain the mechanism and the benefit: scheduling allocates CPU time between competing processes so users receive timely responses, rather than merely saying it “makes it faster”.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>"},{"id":"memory","slr":4,"spec":"1.2.1(b)","title":"Memory management","desc":"Paging, page tables, segmentation, virtual memory and thrashing","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr04-paging-segmentation-and-virtual-memory/","html":"\n\u003ch3>Why memory management is necessary\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The OS must allocate RAM to active processes, keep their address spaces separate, reclaim released memory and translate addresses used by a programme into physical locations. It may also make use of backing storage when available RAM is insufficient.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Paging\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A process's \u003cstrong>virtual address space\u003c/strong> is divided into equal-sized \u003cb>pages\u003c/b>. Physical RAM is divided into same-sized \u003cb>frames\u003c/b>. Pages of the same process need not occupy adjacent frames. A \u003cb>page table\u003c/b> maps virtual page numbers to frames and records relevant status information.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Segmentation\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A process is logically divided into \u003cb>variable-length segments\u003c/b> representing areas such as code, stack or data. Each segment has a base and length; the OS checks boundaries and can map segments into memory. Segmentation can cause external fragmentation and may be combined with paging.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Page translation\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Split a virtual address into a page number and offset. Use the page table to find the physical frame. Keep the offset unchanged. Example: with 1,024-byte pages, virtual address 2,700 lies in page 2 (zero-based) with offset 652. If page 2 maps to frame 7, the physical address is \u003cb>7 × 1,024 + 652 = 7,820\u003c/b>. Try the interactive paging translator.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Virtual memory and swapping\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Virtual memory gives each process a virtual address space. When a required page is not in RAM, a \u003cb>page fault\u003c/b> may cause the OS to obtain it from slower backing storage. Pages may be evicted or written back as needed. Virtual memory is \u003cstrong>not the same\u003c/strong> as the virtual/cloud file storage in H446 1.1.3(d).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Benefit\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Processes can operate with address spaces larger than current physical RAM allocations; the OS can share RAM and isolate processes while loading only required pages.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Moving pages to/from storage is much slower than using RAM. Excessive page faults cause \u003cstrong>thrashing\u003c/strong>, where the computer spends much of its time transferring pages instead of executing useful instructions.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original example: audio workstation\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A music studio runs several virtual instruments and a large sample library. Infrequently used pages may be moved to storage, but when the project repeatedly touches those pages, audio performance can degrade because page faults generate I/O. Adding RAM reduces pressure when there is a genuine working-set shortage.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Precision:\u003c/b> paging is fixed-size and avoids external fragmentation of frames; segmentation uses variable-sized logical regions. Page tables map pages to frames, not necessarily one process to one continuous block.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"interrupts","slr":4,"spec":"1.2.1(c)","title":"Interrupts and ISRs","desc":"Interrupt requests, priorities, saving state and returning to the FDE cycle","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr04-interrupts/","html":"\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>interrupt\u003c/strong> is a signal requesting the processor's attention for an event such as an input becoming ready, a timer expiring or an exception. The CPU can respond without continuously polling every device.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Typical interrupt sequence\u003c/h3>\u003col>\u003cli>A hardware device, clock or software event raises a request. Interrupt control logic records a pending event.\u003c/li>\u003cli>At a suitable instruction boundary (in the simplified FDE model) the CPU checks whether a permitted interrupt is pending and prioritises it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The execution \u003cb>context\u003c/b> is preserved, including the instruction address and any necessary registers/status. A stack can be used.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Control transfers to the \u003cstrong>interrupt service routine (ISR)\u003c/strong> for the relevant interrupt.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The ISR services the event, for example transferring data to a buffer or clearing a request. It must preserve the state required for correct return.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The saved context is restored and the interrupted execution resumes, unless the OS scheduler has selected another process.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"flow\">\u003cspan>Instruction completes\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Pending interrupt?\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Save state\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Run ISR\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Restore / resume\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Priority and nested interrupts\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Higher-priority events can be handled before lower-priority requests when permitted. A higher-priority interrupt may interrupt an ISR, requiring further context to be stored; the \u003cb>last saved\u003c/b> state is typically restored \u003cb>first\u003c/b> (LIFO stack). Not every platform uses identical interrupt handling rules, and a masked interrupt may wait.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original example: a theatre lighting controller\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A timed lighting cue is being calculated when a hardware button requests an immediate emergency blackout. The emergency input generates a high-priority interrupt; the controller preserves its execution state, invokes the blackout ISR, then continues or enters a safe state as required.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Common errors\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An interrupt is not necessarily an error. A scheduler time-slice timer is a normal source of interrupts. The ISR runs in response to an event; it is not the same as the complete operating system.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>"},{"id":"scheduling","slr":4,"spec":"1.2.1(d)","title":"Processor scheduling","desc":"FCFS, RR, SJF, SRT and multi-level feedback queues","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr04-scheduling/","html":"\n\u003cp>Scheduling selects which \u003cb>ready\u003c/b> process should run on the CPU. Its objectives include high CPU utilisation and throughput, fair access, suitable response times and avoiding excessive waiting. On a single-core processor, switching between processes creates the appearance of simultaneous work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Algorithm\u003c/th>\u003cth>How it works\u003c/th>\u003cth>Benefit and limitation\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>FCFS\u003c/b> (First Come First Served)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run jobs in arrival order until they complete or block. Usually non-preemptive.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simple and fair by arrival, but a long job can delay shorter ones (\u003cem>convoy effect\u003c/em>).\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>RR\u003c/b> (Round Robin)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Give each ready job a \u003cem>time quantum\u003c/em>, then cycle to the next. Preempt unfinished jobs after a quantum.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Responsive and fair time sharing, but small quanta lead to more context-switch overhead.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>SJF\u003c/b> (Shortest Job First)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>When CPU becomes free, choose the ready job with the shortest estimated total burst. Non-preemptive.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can reduce average waiting time if estimates are accurate, but long jobs can starve.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>SRT\u003c/b> (Shortest Remaining Time)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run the ready job with the shortest \u003cem>remaining\u003c/em> burst; a shorter arriving job can preempt it.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Responsive to short jobs, but needs estimates, can switch frequently and may starve longer jobs.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>MLFQ\u003c/b> (Multi-Level Feedback Queue)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Several priority queues with differing time quanta. Jobs using much CPU time can move down; long-waiting jobs can be promoted.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Favours interactive jobs and adapts to behaviour, but more complicated and policy-dependent.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003ch3>Core vocabulary\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Preemption\u003c/b>\u003cp>The scheduler removes the CPU from a running process before its CPU burst has ended, often when its time quantum expires or a higher-priority job arrives.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Context switch\u003c/b>\u003cp>Save the current process state and restore another's; the switch incurs time and cache overhead.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Turnaround time\u003c/b>\u003cp>Completion time minus arrival time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Waiting time\u003c/b>\u003cp>Time a process spends waiting in the ready queue; for simple CPU-only jobs, turnaround minus burst length.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Response time\u003c/b>\u003cp>First time scheduled minus arrival time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Starvation\u003c/b>\u003cp>A process waits excessively or indefinitely because others keep being favoured. Ageing is a possible remedy.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Fresh workload\u003c/strong>\u003cp>Three jobs arrive at time 0: Orchid (5 cycles), Birch (2 cycles), Maple (3 cycles). \u003cb>FCFS\u003c/b> in listed order completes at times 5, 7 and 10. \u003cb>SJF\u003c/b> runs Birch → Maple → Orchid, with completions 2, 5 and 10. \u003cb>RR\u003c/b> with a 2-cycle quantum rotates between unfinished jobs. Compare their exact timelines using the live scheduler.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>MLFQ note:\u003c/b> there is no single universal MLFQ algorithm. Exam answers should explain priorities, multiple queues, demotion and potential promotion rather than assume a fixed quantum policy.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"os-types","slr":4,"spec":"1.2.1(e)","title":"Types of operating system","desc":"Distributed, embedded, multitasking, multi-user and real-time","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr04-types-of-operating-system/","html":"\n\u003ch3>Choose by the needs of the system\u003c/h3>\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Type\u003c/th>\u003cth>Key features\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical suitable use\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Distributed\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Coordinates resources and tasks on multiple networked computers, presenting them as one logical system where supported.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A large astronomy-processing installation that divides observations across networked nodes.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Embedded\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Designed for a specific device with limited resources and often predictable tasks; firmware and OS may be tightly integrated.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A smart irrigation controller with a small display and a few sensors.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Multitasking\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Supports several processes through scheduling and time sharing, possibly across several cores.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A laptop handling a music player, editor and backups.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Multi-user\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Supports concurrent users with separate accounts, protection and resource allocation.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A university central system serving independent logins.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Real-time (RTOS)\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Designed to meet defined \u003cem>timing deadlines\u003c/em> predictably; correctness depends on both the result and when it arrives.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An industrial emergency shutdown controller.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003ch3>Hard and soft real time\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>Hard real-time:\u003c/b> a missed deadline is unacceptable and may cause failure or danger. \u003cb>Soft real-time:\u003c/b> missed deadlines reduce quality, but may be tolerable. \u003cstrong>Real time means predictable deadlines, not simply a fast processor.\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Overlapping characteristics\u003c/h3>\u003cp>These categories are not mutually exclusive. An embedded system can also run a real-time OS; a multi-user server can be multitasking. A distributed OS specifically coordinates multiple machines, whereas an ordinary networked OS may only connect to shared services.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original scenario\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A coastal monitoring centre uses a single-purpose tide-gate controller that must respond within a fixed deadline to a dangerous water-level reading. An embedded real-time design is appropriate because reliability and bounded response are more important than running a wide selection of applications.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>"},{"id":"bios","slr":4,"spec":"1.2.1(f)","title":"BIOS and booting","desc":"Firmware, POST, bootstrapping and modern UEFI","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr04-bios/","html":"\n\u003cp>\u003cb>BIOS\u003c/b> stands for Basic Input/Output System. It is non-volatile firmware that starts when the computer receives power, initialises essential hardware and begins booting the operating system. On many modern PCs, \u003cb>UEFI\u003c/b> provides newer firmware features; BIOS remains an OCR specification term.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"flow\">\u003cspan>Power on\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Firmware initialisation\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Hardware checks (POST)\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Boot loader\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>OS in RAM\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n\u003col>\u003cli>Firmware starts executing from non-volatile storage.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Initialises CPU, memory and basic devices; runs checks such as the Power-On Self-Test (POST).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Selects a bootable device according to firmware settings and boot configuration.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Transfers control to boot-loader code which finds and loads the OS kernel into RAM.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The operating system sets up services and device drivers and presents a login or other interface.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original example\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A library's catalogue PC shows a firmware configuration screen before the OS appears. This demonstrates that firmware starts before the operating system and does not depend on the OS to initialise essential hardware.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Exam distinction:\u003c/b> BIOS/UEFI firmware is not the same as the operating system. The firmware begins the boot process; the boot loader/firmware hand-off then loads and starts the OS. Modern machines may have a different sequence from older BIOS-only designs.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"drivers","slr":4,"spec":"1.2.1(g)","title":"Device drivers","desc":"How software controls specific hardware and handles I/O","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr04-device-drivers/","html":"\n\u003cp>A \u003cb>device driver\u003c/b> is systems software that provides an interface between an operating system and a particular piece or family of hardware. It translates generic requests into the device-specific operations the hardware understands.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"flow\">\u003cspan>Application request\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>OS interface\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Device driver\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Hardware\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>What a driver does\u003c/h3>\u003cul>\u003cli>Receives commands from the OS and issues device-specific instructions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>May initialise the device and configure its modes, buffers and communication protocol.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Receives status and errors from hardware and reports information back to the OS.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Handles device data transfer and may work with interrupts or DMA.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Provides a consistent software interface so applications need not understand device-specific register layouts.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch3>Why versions matter\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A driver needs to match the OS interface, hardware model and sometimes CPU architecture. Missing or incompatible drivers can prevent a device functioning fully. Drivers can operate with significant privileges, so installing trusted, maintained versions matters.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original example\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A design studio connects a specialist pen plotter. An application issues a standard print command, but the driver converts it into the commands and timing expected by that plotter, including pen movement and media settings.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Driver compared with firmware\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Firmware is software stored on or closely associated with hardware. A device driver typically runs under the operating system to communicate with it. A device may have both.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>"},{"id":"vm","slr":4,"spec":"1.2.1(h)","title":"Virtual machines","desc":"Hosted operating systems, virtualisation and bytecode execution","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr04-virtual-machines/","html":"\n\u003cp>A \u003cb>virtual machine (VM)\u003c/b> is software that creates a computing environment acting like a machine. The OCR requirement includes both running another operating system and executing intermediate code.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>System VM\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A \u003cb>hypervisor\u003c/b> provides virtual CPU, memory, storage and network devices for a guest operating system. The host can run several isolated guest systems on one physical machine.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Example:\u003c/b> a software workshop runs a Linux guest on a Windows host to test an application in a different environment.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Process or language VM\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A language runtime executes intermediate instructions such as \u003cb>bytecode\u003c/b>. A Java programme may compile to Java bytecode, then run via a Java Virtual Machine with an interpreter or just-in-time compiler.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Example:\u003c/b> one set of bytecode files can run on systems with a compatible JVM.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Why use virtualisation?\u003c/h3>\u003cul>\u003cli>Hardware consolidation and more flexible allocation of CPU, memory and storage.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Testing and deployment across different environments without altering the physical host.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Isolation between environments to limit interference; snapshots and disposable environments can aid recovery.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Portability for intermediate code when a compatible runtime exists.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Trade-offs:\u003c/b> VMs have resource overhead, require management and can have compatibility issues. Isolation is not an absolute guarantee against malware: configuration flaws and VM escapes are possible. A language VM is not automatically a complete sandbox.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original example\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A geography research group wants to test software built for another operating system. They install a VM rather than repartitioning their main computers, accepting the additional memory and CPU overhead for the convenience of a separate guest environment.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>"},{"id":"apps","slr":5,"spec":"1.2.2(a)","title":"Applications and choosing software","desc":"Systems versus applications; general-purpose, special-purpose, bespoke","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr05-the-nature-of-applications/","html":"\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Systems software\u003c/b> operates and maintains the computing environment, including operating systems, drivers, utilities and translators. \u003cb>Application software\u003c/b> helps users perform tasks such as editing text, designing models, analysing data or communicating.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Types of application\u003c/h3>\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Type\u003c/th>\u003cth>Description\u003c/th>\u003cth>Consideration\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>General purpose\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Used for a range of tasks, e.g. a spreadsheet or word processor.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Flexible and familiar but may lack specialist features.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Special purpose\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Designed for a limited area, e.g. software for managing theatre seating.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Efficient for that domain but less flexible elsewhere.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Bespoke\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Custom-designed for one customer or organisation.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can match exact workflows, but development and maintenance cost more.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Off-the-shelf\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Existing software sold or distributed to many customers.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often quicker and cheaper to deploy, but might need workarounds.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003ch3>How to justify a software selection\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Match requirements to \u003cb>functionality, hardware and OS compatibility, file formats, accessibility, security/privacy, reliability, user support, licensing, total cost of ownership, availability, updates and maintainability\u003c/b>. The same application may not be the right choice for every organisation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original scenario: heritage volunteer rota\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A small heritage site needs a rota for 30 volunteers, exports to spreadsheets and low ongoing cost. A supported off-the-shelf rota tool may be sensible because setup is quick and features already exist. A bespoke system becomes attractive if complex role restrictions or integrations cannot be met by existing products.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Exam technique:\u003c/b> name a specific requirement, state a relevant feature of the software, and explain how that feature meets the requirement. “It is good” is not a justification.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"utilities","slr":5,"spec":"1.2.2(b)","title":"Utility software","desc":"Security, backup, compression, updating and disk maintenance","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr05-utilities/","html":"\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Utility software\u003c/b> maintains, manages, protects or optimises a computer system. Some utilities are bundled into the OS; others are installed separately.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Backup and recovery\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Creates copies of selected files, scheduled automatically if needed. A sound strategy considers frequency, location, recovery time, versioning, encryption and testing restores. A synchronised folder alone is not necessarily a backup.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Antivirus and anti-malware\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Detects and blocks threats using signatures, heuristics, behaviour analysis and other techniques. Needs updates and may produce false positives or miss new threats.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Compression\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Reduces file size for storage or transmission. Lossless compression allows exact reconstruction, while lossy methods discard some information for potentially greater size reduction.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Automatic updates\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Checks for and installs patches. Security fixes close vulnerabilities; other updates improve functionality or stability. Updates can introduce compatibility issues and may need staged deployment.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Disk management\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Defragmenting an HDD arranges scattered blocks to reduce mechanical seeking. Flash SSDs have no moving heads and instead use controller wear-levelling and TRIM; routine HDD-style defragmentation is not normally helpful.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Other utilities\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Disk cleanup, file encryption, firewall management, diagnostics, integrity checking and resource monitoring support system operation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original scenario: wildlife media project\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A research group records thousands of audio clips. Scheduled, versioned backups protect irreplaceable field recordings; compression helps distribute datasets; endpoint protection reduces malware risks; disk health monitoring alerts the team to failing storage.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Distinguish:\u003c/b> a utility manages the system; an application completes a user's end task. A programme can be useful without being classed as utility software.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"licences","slr":5,"spec":"1.2.2(c)","title":"Open-source and closed-source software","desc":"Access to source, rights, licensing and trade-offs","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr05-open-vs-closed/","html":"\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Open-source\u003c/b> software makes source code available under a licence permitting use, inspection, modification and redistribution subject to that licence's terms. \u003cb>Closed-source\u003c/b> software generally does not give users the source code or broad modification rights.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Dimension\u003c/th>\u003cth>Open source\u003c/th>\u003cth>Closed source\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Source access\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Available under the licence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Usually not provided\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Modification\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Permitted subject to conditions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Usually controlled by the owner\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Support\u003c/td>\u003ctd>May be community, commercial or both\u003c/td>\u003ctd>May be offered by the vendor and partners\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>May be free or commercially supported\u003c/td>\u003ctd>May be paid or free-of-charge\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Risks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Maintainer availability, licence obligations, support variation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Vendor dependence, closed formats, limited customisation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003ch3>Licences matter\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Some \u003cb>permissive\u003c/b> licences allow redistribution with comparatively few restrictions; \u003cb>copyleft\u003c/b> licences place conditions on redistribution of modified or derived software. Therefore it is wrong to say that all open-source code must always be redistributed under the same licence. \u003cb>Freeware\u003c/b> describes price, not a guarantee of open source.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Choosing fairly\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Open source can help audit, localisation and customisation. A closed-source product might provide integrated paid support, specialist features and contractual service commitments. Either model can be secure or insecure, depending on maintenance and configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original scenario: community translation tool\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A multilingual charity wants to add an uncommon language pack. A suitably licensed open-source application might allow volunteers to contribute translations, but the charity must check deployment support, accessibility and ongoing security maintenance before adopting it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>"},{"id":"translators","slr":5,"spec":"1.2.2(d)","title":"Assemblers, compilers and interpreters","desc":"Translation strategies, source/object code, bytecode and portability","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr05-translators/","html":"\n\u003cp>Processors execute machine instructions, not high-level source text. A \u003cb>translator\u003c/b> converts instructions into a representation suitable for execution, sometimes producing machine code and sometimes an intermediate form.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Assembler\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Translates symbolic low-level \u003cb>assembly\u003c/b> mnemonics and labels into machine code for a particular instruction set. Usually there is a close correspondence between assembly operations and machine instructions.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Compiler\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Analyses and translates a source programme (or source modules) before execution, producing machine/object code or an intermediate representation. It can perform checking and optimisation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Interpreter\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Executes a programme by reading/evaluating instructions at run time, often with no separately distributed native executable. Many modern systems interpret bytecode or combine interpretation with just-in-time compilation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch3>Bytecode and runtime\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>Bytecode\u003c/b> is intermediate code that can be executed or compiled further by a compatible \u003cb>virtual machine\u003c/b>. It separates the source-language compiler from the underlying machine architecture.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Comparison\u003c/th>\u003cth>Compiled ahead of time\u003c/th>\u003cth>Interpreted at runtime\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Execution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Usually starts from an already-translated programme\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Requires an interpreter or runtime to evaluate code\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Performance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often optimised for quick repeated execution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can be slower for repeated operations, although modern JITs can be fast\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Portability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Native binary is often platform-specific\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Source or bytecode may run with an appropriate runtime\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Debugging\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many problems can be detected before execution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Convenient for experimenting and step-by-step execution\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original example: language runtime\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A board-game scoring tool is written in a high-level language, compiled into bytecode and distributed to different machines. Each machine needs a compatible runtime to execute that bytecode. This is portability through a shared intermediate representation, not a guarantee that every platform supports every feature.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Important nuance:\u003c/b> calling Python “interpreted” or C++ “compiled” is useful at A Level, but real implementations can combine techniques. A compiler need not always generate a native executable directly; it may produce object modules or bytecode first.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"compilation","slr":5,"spec":"1.2.2(e)","title":"The stages of compilation","desc":"Lexing, parsing, semantic checks, code generation and optimisation","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr05-stages-of-compilation/","html":"\n\u003cp>A compiler translates source into target code using several logical passes. OCR specifically requires \u003cb>lexical analysis, syntax analysis, code generation and optimisation\u003c/b>; understanding \u003cb>symbol tables and semantic checking\u003c/b> helps explain the complete process.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"flow\">\u003cspan>Source text\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Lexer\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Parser\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Semantic checks\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Code generation\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Optimisation\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>1. Lexical analysis\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The lexer groups characters into meaningful \u003cb>tokens\u003c/b> such as identifiers, keywords, literals, operators and punctuation. Comments and irrelevant whitespace are normally ignored. The lexer can detect invalid characters or malformed literal tokens.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>New tokenisation example\u003c/strong>\u003cp>Code: \u003ccode>total = 8 * count\u003c/code>. One possible token stream: \u003ccode>IDENT(total)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ASSIGN(=)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>INTEGER(8)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>MULTIPLY(*)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>IDENT(count)\u003c/code>. Exact token names depend on the language/compiler.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>2. Syntax analysis (parsing)\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The parser checks whether the token sequence fits the language's grammar, often constructing a \u003cb>parse tree or abstract syntax tree (AST)\u003c/b>. A missing closing bracket or invalid ordering can cause a syntax error.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>3. Semantic checks and symbol table\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The compiler checks whether the programme makes sense according to language rules: identifiers are declared where required, types are compatible and names resolve to permitted symbols. A \u003cb>symbol table\u003c/b> stores attributes of names such as identifier, type and scope; later passes use it to allocate or resolve storage. It is not simply a list of all keywords, and symbol information may be collected at several stages.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>4. Code generation\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The compiler creates target instructions or an intermediate representation from the validated programme. Different target CPUs require different native instruction sets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>5. Code optimisation\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Optimisation preserves required programme behaviour while reducing unnecessary work or improving speed/memory use. Examples include \u003cb>constant folding\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>6 * 7\u003c/code> → \u003ccode>42\u003c/code> when safe) and removing unreachable code. Optimisation can occur before, during or after code generation and can increase compilation time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Lexical failure\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An unterminated quoted string may not form a valid token.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Syntax failure\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An expression with an incomplete bracket structure may not match the grammar.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Semantic failure\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assigning a string to a strictly typed numeric variable may be syntactically valid but type-invalid.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003ch4>Runtime or logic failure\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Source may compile successfully yet divide by zero at execution or produce an incorrect algorithmic result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use the \u003cb>Compiler Pipeline Lab\u003c/b> to inspect the transformations and classify errors with independent examples.\u003c/p>"},{"id":"linking","slr":5,"spec":"1.2.2(f)","title":"Linkers, loaders and libraries","desc":"Reusable routines, object modules, relocation and running an executable","video":"https://craigndave.org/videos/ocr-alevel-slr05-linkers-loaders-libraries/","html":"\n\u003ch3>Libraries: reusing tested functionality\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>library\u003c/strong> is a collection of prewritten routines a programme can call. Libraries reduce development time, promote reuse and consistency, and may provide specialist operations. Dependencies need checking for compatibility, licences, security updates and version changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Linker\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Compilation can produce \u003cb>object files\u003c/b> containing machine instructions plus unresolved references to other routines. A \u003cb>linker\u003c/b> combines object modules, resolves references to external functions and incorporates or references required libraries to produce a programme ready for loading.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Static and dynamic linking\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"pair\">\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Static linking\u003c/b>\u003cp>Required library code is included in the resulting executable. The file can be larger but has fewer separately deployed library dependencies.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"note\">\u003cb>Dynamic linking\u003c/b>\u003cp>The executable refers to libraries provided separately at load time or runtime. Different programmes can share them, though a missing or incompatible version may prevent execution.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch3>Loader\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The OS loader locates the programme on secondary storage, maps executable sections into RAM/virtual memory, prepares the process environment, performs required relocations and dynamic linking work, and transfers control to the programme entry point.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"flow\">\u003cspan>Source module A + B\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Compiler / assembler\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Object files\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Linker + libraries\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Executable\u003c/span>\u003cb>→\u003c/b>\u003cspan>Loader\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"worked\">\u003cstrong>Original example: scientific charting programme\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A weather-research application is compiled as separate graphing and data-reading modules. The linker resolves a call to a statistics library, and the loader prepares the executable and its runtime dependencies in memory before the first instruction runs.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cb>Keep roles separate:\u003c/b> a translator generates target code; the linker connects references/modules; the loader prepares the executable for running. The exact responsibility for relocation and shared libraries can vary by operating system and executable format.\u003c/p>"}],"vocab":[["Operating system","System software that manages hardware/resources and provides services for applications.",4],["GUI","Graphical user interface using controls such as windows, menus and icons.",4],["CLI","Command-line interface accepting typed commands and scripts.",4],["Memory management","OS allocation, translation and protection of processes' working memory.",4],["Paging","Dividing virtual memory into fixed-size pages and RAM into corresponding frames.",4],["Page frame","A fixed-size physical RAM unit able to hold one virtual page.",4],["Page table","Mapping between virtual page numbers and physical frames, with additional status information.",4],["Page fault","An access to a page requiring OS intervention, often because the page is not resident.",4],["Segmentation","Dividing memory into logical segments of potentially different lengths.",4],["External fragmentation","Free space broken into separated regions that may not fit a large segment.",4],["Virtual memory","OS memory management using a virtual address space and potentially backing storage.",4],["Thrashing","Excessive page movement that leaves little time for useful execution.",4],["Interrupt","A request for the processor to attend to an event.",4],["ISR","Interrupt Service Routine: code that responds to a particular interrupt.",4],["Context switch","Saving the running process state and restoring another process's state.",4],["Interrupt priority","A relative urgency used to select which pending interrupt is serviced.",4],["Stack","A last-in, first-out structure for saving and restoring items, including context.",4],["Processor scheduling","Choosing which ready process receives CPU time.",4],["Preemption","Removing a process from the CPU before it has completed its CPU burst.",4],["Time quantum","Maximum consecutive CPU time allocated to a process in time-sharing.",4],["Round Robin (RR)","A rotating ready queue with time quanta for processes.",4],["First Come First Served (FCFS)","Processes run in arrival order, generally without preemption.",4],["Shortest Job First (SJF)","Non-preemptive selection of the ready process with shortest expected burst.",4],["Shortest Remaining Time (SRT)","Preemptive scheduling of the ready process with least remaining CPU burst.",4],["Multi-Level Feedback Queue (MLFQ)","Several priority queues with adjustment based on process behaviour or waiting.",4],["Starvation","A process waits for CPU service indefinitely or excessively.",4],["Turnaround time","A job's completion time minus its arrival time.",4],["Waiting time","Total time a process remains ready but not running.",4],["Response time","Time until a process first obtains CPU service minus arrival time.",4],["Distributed OS","Coordinates resources across multiple networked computers as one logical system.",4],["Embedded OS","An OS tailored for a particular device and limited functions/resources.",4],["Multitasking OS","An OS that manages concurrent progress of multiple processes.",4],["Multi-user OS","An OS that supports several users with protected, shared resources.",4],["Real-time OS","An OS that prioritises predictably meeting specified timing deadlines.",4],["Hard real time","A deadline must be met for correct operation.",4],["Soft real time","A missed deadline reduces service quality but may be tolerated.",4],["BIOS","Basic Input/Output System firmware that initialises hardware and starts booting.",4],["POST","Power-On Self-Test performed by firmware during start-up.",4],["Boot loader","Code that locates, loads and starts an operating system.",4],["UEFI","Modern firmware interface replacing traditional PC BIOS in many systems.",4],["Device driver","Software that translates OS requests into hardware-specific operations.",4],["Virtual machine","Software environment that acts like a machine or runtime for another system/code.",4],["Hypervisor","Software providing virtual hardware and managing guest VMs.",4],["Guest operating system","OS running within a system virtual machine.",4],["Bytecode","Intermediate instructions for a compatible language runtime or VM.",4],["Application software","Software designed to perform tasks for a user.",5],["Systems software","Software supporting operation and maintenance of the computer.",5],["General-purpose software","An application usable for a wide range of tasks.",5],["Special-purpose software","An application designed for a limited domain or task.",5],["Bespoke software","Software custom developed for a particular customer's requirements.",5],["Off-the-shelf software","An existing software product distributed to multiple customers.",5],["Utility software","Software for maintaining, managing or protecting a system.",5],["Automatic backup","Utility that schedules copies of data for recovery.",5],["Antivirus","Utility that detects or blocks malicious software using signatures and heuristics.",5],["Compression","Reduction of data size for storage or transmission.",5],["Disk defragmentation","Rearranging fragmented file blocks, mainly to reduce HDD mechanical seeks.",5],["Open source","Licensed software whose source is available with specified use/modification rights.",5],["Closed source","Software whose source and modification rights are usually controlled by its owner.",5],["Freeware","Software distributed without a usage charge, not necessarily with source code.",5],["Copyleft","Licensing conditions that may require derivative distributions to preserve freedoms.",5],["Proprietary software","Software whose usage and distribution are controlled by the copyright holder.",5],["Source code","Human-readable programme instructions before translation.",5],["Object code","Translated machine instructions or modules that may need linking.",5],["Translator","Software that transforms source instructions into another executable representation.",5],["Assembler","Translator from assembly mnemonics into processor machine code.",5],["Compiler","Translator that analyses and generates target code before its normal execution.",5],["Interpreter","Runtime that reads/evaluates programme instructions as they execute.",5],["JIT compilation","Translation of code into machine instructions during programme execution.",5],["Lexical analysis","Grouping source characters into meaningful tokens.",5],["Token","A classified language unit such as identifier, literal or operator.",5],["Syntax analysis","Parsing a token stream to check grammar and construct a tree.",5],["AST","Abstract Syntax Tree: a structured representation of programme syntax.",5],["Semantic analysis","Checking type and identifier meaning after syntax is understood.",5],["Symbol table","Compiler record of identifiers and their attributes such as type and scope.",5],["Code generation","Producing target instructions or an intermediate representation.",5],["Optimisation","Improving efficiency while preserving required behaviour.",5],["Constant folding","Calculating constant expressions during compilation when safe.",5],["Linker","Combines object modules and resolves external references and libraries.",5],["Loader","Prepares and maps an executable into memory ready for execution.",5],["Static linking","Including required library code in the linked executable.",5],["Dynamic linking","Resolving libraries separately at load time or runtime.",5],["Software library","Reusable functions or components called by a programme.",5]],"q":[["os-role","Which component normally provides a hardware-independent interface to applications?",["Operating system","Word processor","Printed document","Monitor panel"],0,"The OS provides standard services above the hardware."],["os-role","Why is an OS needed when several programmes run?",["To allocate and coordinate shared resources","To eliminate all device interrupts","To remove RAM","To make all applications open-source"],0,"Resource management allows concurrent programmes to operate safely."],["os-role","Which is a benefit of a CLI?",["Convenient scripting and automation","All commands are visible as icons","No commands must be learned","Every task becomes graphical"],0,"Command-line interfaces can be automated with scripts."],["os-role","Which OS function handles file permissions and folder organisation?",["File management","Code optimisation","Lexical analysis","Laser printing"],0,"File management organises access and storage."],["os-role","What does an OS scheduler allocate?",["CPU time to ready processes","All user passwords to the network","Pixels to a screen only","Machine opcodes to syntax trees"],0,"The scheduler decides which ready task uses CPU time."],["os-role","What is the role of the OS relative to user programmes?",["Provide services and manage hardware","Replace every application","Run only firmware","Convert mains AC to DC"],0,"The OS mediates access to system resources."],["memory","In paging, virtual pages are mapped to…",["RAM frames","Printer cartridges","CPU opcodes","Network domains"],0,"Fixed-size virtual pages are mapped into physical frames."],["memory","With 256-byte pages, virtual address 770 lies in which zero-based page?",["2","3","4","770"],1,"770 // 256 = 3, with offset 2."],["memory","What is retained when translating a page into a frame?",["Offset within the page","Original virtual page number as physical frame","Operating system version","File name"],0,"The offset is the same inside the physical frame."],["memory","Which feature is characteristic of segmentation?",["Variable-size logical memory regions","Only fixed-size pages","No memory protection","Every segment is a byte"],0,"Segments represent variable-size logical regions."],["memory","What may cause thrashing?",["Repeated page transfers because working memory exceeds RAM","A working set fitting entirely in RAM","A printer printing too quickly","An unused network cable"],0,"Excessive paging can consume much of the processor's time in I/O."],["memory","What does a page table provide?",["Virtual-to-physical page mapping","A list of all keyboard shortcuts","Printer colour settings","Compile-time tokens only"],0,"Page tables identify where virtual pages are mapped."],["memory","What is a page fault?",["An access requiring memory-management intervention","A failed printer tray","A syntax error in Python","An optical disc defect"],0,"It occurs when address translation or residency requires OS action."],["interrupts","What is an ISR?",["Routine handling a particular interrupt","Instruction storage register","An ordinary user document","A linker module only"],0,"ISRs respond to relevant interrupt events."],["interrupts","Why save processor context before servicing an interrupt?",["To resume the interrupted computation correctly","To make firmware faster forever","To clear RAM","To encrypt keyboard input"],0,"Preserving registers and PC allows correct resumption."],["interrupts","Which event could trigger a normal interrupt?",["Timer tick","A green wallpaper","A completed printout stored on paper only","A larger monitor bezel"],0,"OS timers commonly generate interrupts."],["interrupts","What is the main role of an interrupt priority?",["Select urgent events for attention","Make all devices identical","Prevent any multitasking","Optimise source syntax"],0,"Priority can determine service order."],["interrupts","Why is a stack useful for nested interrupts?",["Last saved context is restored first","It sorts all files","It stores only videos","It replaces the CPU"],0,"LIFO supports nested saving/restoring."],["interrupts","When are interrupts typically checked in a simplified FDE model?",["At an instruction boundary","Only when the computer boots","Only during compilation","Only if RAM is empty"],0,"Interrupts are generally accepted at a safe point in execution."],["scheduling","Which algorithm runs ready jobs in arrival order without ordinary time slicing?",["FCFS","SRT","MLFQ","RR"],0,"FCFS selects in order of arrival."],["scheduling","What is the time limit given to a job under RR?",["Time quantum","Page offset","Bytecode","Segment base"],0,"Each job receives a time slice."],["scheduling","Which scheduling policy can interrupt a long-running job when a shorter new job arrives?",["SRT","Non-preemptive SJF","FCFS","Static linking"],0,"SRT compares remaining times and can preempt."],["scheduling","Which policy chooses the shortest estimated ready job when CPU becomes free?",["SJF","Round Robin","FCFS always","FIFO disk queue only"],0,"SJF is non-preemptive and chooses a short job."],["scheduling","What is a key feature of MLFQ?",["Jobs can move between priority queues","Every job remains permanently in one queue","No priorities exist","All tasks execute simultaneously on one core"],0,"MLFQ adapts priorities based on behaviour."],["scheduling","What does starvation mean?",["A job is delayed indefinitely by scheduler choices","The CPU is switched off","A source file is deleted","A stack overflows"],0,"Some priority systems can repeatedly postpone jobs."],["scheduling","For a job arriving at time 3 and finishing at time 13, turnaround is…",["10 time units","13 time units","3 time units","16 time units"],0,"Turnaround = completion − arrival = 10."],["scheduling","Which change generally increases RR context-switch overhead?",["Using a much shorter quantum","Using longer quanta only","Increasing disk capacity","Adding a keyboard"],0,"More frequent switches cost time."],["os-types","Which OS is chosen primarily for predictable deadline handling?",["Real-time","Multi-user only","Distributed only","Batch file"],0,"RTOS scheduling supports bounded response time."],["os-types","An OS designed for one specialised sensor appliance is typically…",["Embedded","General-purpose multi-user server only","A compiler","A library"],0,"Embedded systems focus on particular hardware and tasks."],["os-types","Which OS type coordinates processing across multiple computers as one system?",["Distributed OS","Single-user text editor","Assembler","Device driver"],0,"A distributed OS manages networked resources."],["os-types","Can an embedded OS also be real-time?",["Yes","Never","Only on the Internet","Only with a keyboard"],0,"Operating-system categories overlap."],["os-types","What is the main meaning of hard real time?",["Deadlines must be met for correct operation","The CPU must have high GHz","Only cloud storage is used","No interrupts exist"],0,"Correctness depends on reliable timing."],["bios","Which typically begins system initialisation before the OS loads?",["Firmware such as BIOS/UEFI","A word processor","A printer driver inside an application","The browser cache"],0,"Firmware runs at the start of the boot sequence."],["bios","What does POST check?",["Basic hardware functionality at start-up","Python source formatting","A database's search results","A programme's mark scheme"],0,"Power-On Self-Test is a firmware start-up check."],["bios","What loads and starts the operating system kernel?",["Boot-loader/firmware boot sequence","The screen's backlight","A linker alone","A spreadsheet cell"],0,"The boot loader locates and starts the operating system."],["bios","Where is firmware generally stored?",["Non-volatile memory","Unpowered RAM only","The accumulator","Only remote virtual storage"],0,"Firmware must persist when power is off."],["bios","Which modern firmware interface frequently replaces traditional PC BIOS?",["UEFI","ASCII","ALU","FCFS"],0,"UEFI is modern PC firmware."],["drivers","What is a device driver's main role?",["Translate OS operations to hardware-specific commands","Create source tokens","Build the application UI","Replace every OS service"],0,"Drivers connect generic OS interfaces to particular hardware."],["drivers","Why might an incompatible driver prevent hardware working?",["It cannot correctly communicate with the OS/device","The graphics card has no software at all","All hardware uses identical commands","Every driver is processor-independent"],0,"Drivers depend on hardware and OS interfaces."],["drivers","Which process typically requests device services through the OS?",["Application software","An optical disc surface","A paper document","Power socket"],0,"The OS/driver handle device requests."],["drivers","Why should device drivers be kept updated through trusted sources?",["Security and compatibility","To remove all interrupts","To make all programmes portable","To stop hardware using electricity"],0,"Privileged driver faults can create stability and security risks."],["drivers","Is device firmware the same as an OS device driver?",["No","Yes, always","Only for printers","Only for keyboards"],0,"Firmware runs on/with the hardware; drivers normally run under the OS."],["vm","What software controls a system virtual machine's virtual resources?",["Hypervisor","Lexical analyser","Print spooler only","Disk defragmenter"],0,"A hypervisor presents virtual hardware to guests."],["vm","What is a guest OS?",["An OS running inside a VM","The host computer monitor","A printer cable","A source code comment"],0,"Guest OS runs against virtual hardware."],["vm","Which code representation is often run by a language VM?",["Bytecode","A DVD","Printer ink","Only BIOS"],0,"Intermediate bytecode can be executed by a compatible runtime."],["vm","Is virtualisation guaranteed protection from every malicious programme?",["No","Yes","Only if more RAM is installed","Only on all cloud systems"],0,"Isolation helps but depends on correct configuration and implementation."],["vm","What may be a disadvantage of running several guests?",["Memory/CPU and management overhead","Automatic elimination of all bugs","Instant hard real-time guarantees","Removal of all security needs"],0,"VMs use host resources and require management."],["vm","Why can bytecode be portable?",["A compatible runtime can translate/execute it on each platform","All CPUs execute identical binary machine code","Bytecode is human-readable only","It always runs without libraries"],0,"A compatible VM/runtime implements the intermediate instruction set."],["apps","A system that helps people prepare invoices is usually…",["Application software","Firmware","BIOS","A device driver"],0,"It provides a user-facing task."],["apps","Which type is written to one customer's exact workflow?",["Bespoke","Generic firmware","A CPU register","A single printer driver"],0,"Bespoke software is custom-developed."],["apps","What is a potential benefit of off-the-shelf software?",["Faster initial deployment","Guaranteed perfect fit","Unlimited customisation for free","No licence terms"],0,"Existing solutions may already include required features."],["apps","Which matters when selecting a software product?",["Compatibility, accessibility and total cost","Only its logo","Only file size","Only how old it is"],0,"Practical requirements guide the choice."],["apps","What characterises general-purpose software?",["It supports many different tasks","It runs only on one special-purpose machine","It must have no source code","It is always an OS"],0,"General-purpose tools are flexible."],["apps","Which of these is systems software?",["Device driver","Presentation software","Photo editor","Recipe planner"],0,"A driver provides a systems-level hardware interface."],["utilities","Which utility automatically creates recoverable copies of data?",["Backup software","Assembler","Spreadsheet","Type checker"],0,"Backup tools protect against loss."],["utilities","What may an antivirus false positive mean?",["Harmless software is incorrectly flagged","A virus was definitely removed","A file is certainly secure","A printer has no ink"],0,"Heuristics and signatures can misclassify items."],["utilities","What is lossless compression?",["A size reduction allowing exact reconstruction","A guaranteed reduction of every file by half","Deleting information permanently","CPU context saving"],0,"Lossless methods reconstruct the original data."],["utilities","What does an automatic updater mainly do?",["Install patches and new versions","Compile Python to object code","Map virtual addresses","Format all disks"],0,"Updates can fix security and compatibility issues."],["utilities","Which action is more appropriate for maintaining a flash SSD?",["Using TRIM/drive-aware management rather than routine HDD-style defragmentation","Repeatedly forcing full HDD-style defragmentation","Removing all backups","Deleting boot firmware"],0,"Flash has no mechanical seeking and uses wear levelling."],["utilities","What is a key feature of a reliable backup strategy?",["Test restores and retention rules","Only one synchronised folder","No copies outside the computer","Unverified backups"],0,"Recovery must actually be possible."],["licences","Which generally allows examination and modification of source under licence terms?",["Open source","Closed source only","A bytecode file only","A boot loader"],0,"Open-source licences provide rights to inspect and modify."],["licences","Does a price of £0 mean the source must be available?",["No","Yes","Only on Linux","Only for games"],0,"Freeware can remain closed-source."],["licences","Does every open-source licence demand release of all derived source under the same licence?",["No","Yes, always","Only for images","Only on servers"],0,"Different licences carry different redistribution conditions."],["licences","What risk might a proprietary package introduce?",["Vendor lock-in","Automatic ownership of source code","Guaranteed community maintenance","No support option ever"],0,"Closed platforms may constrain switching/customisation."],["licences","What should a school check before redistributing modified software?",["Its licence conditions","Only the executable name","Only CPU speed","Only RAM size"],0,"Modification and redistribution are governed by licence terms."],["licences","Can commercial support be purchased for open-source software?",["Yes","No","Only for closed-source products","Only if all files are secret"],0,"Open-source licensing does not prevent paid support."],["translators","Which translator converts assembly mnemonics into machine instructions?",["Assembler","Disk driver","Linker only","Antivirus"],0,"An assembler translates assembly."],["translators","Which technique typically analyses source ahead of ordinary execution?",["Compilation","Optical storage","CPU scheduling","Monitoring"],0,"Compilation generates code before the translated programme normally runs."],["translators","What executes instructions as a programme runs?",["Interpreter or runtime","Only a printer","Only BIOS","Only a static archive"],0,"An interpreter evaluates code at runtime."],["translators","What is object code?",["Translated instruction code that may still require linking","An input device","A spreadsheet formula only","A software licence"],0,"Compiled modules can contain machine instructions and unresolved references."],["translators","Which is often a benefit of a compatible bytecode runtime?",["Portability across platforms","No need for any software dependencies","Every machine uses the same machine code","Guaranteed perfect speed"],0,"Compatible runtimes implement a shared intermediate representation."],["translators","Are interpreters and compilers always mutually exclusive in real runtimes?",["No","Yes","Only under Windows","Only in databases"],0,"Many systems combine interpretation and JIT compilation."],["translators","What does JIT stand for?",["Just-In-Time","Java Interpreter Table","Joint Instruction Type","Job Interrupt Trigger"],0,"JIT translates code into native instructions during execution."],["compilation","Which stage groups characters into identifiers, operators and literals?",["Lexical analysis","Linking","Loading","Interrupt servicing"],0,"Lexers produce tokens from source text."],["compilation","Which stage checks a token stream against grammar?",["Syntax analysis","Disk scheduling","Loading","Antivirus"],0,"The parser checks syntax and often builds an AST."],["compilation","Which structure stores identifier type and scope information?",["Symbol table","Ready queue","Printer spool","Page frame"],0,"The compiler's symbol table records identifier attributes."],["compilation","Where is incompatible type usage commonly checked?",["Semantic analysis","Only loading","Only CPU instruction fetch","Only disk formatting"],0,"Semantic analysis checks program meaning such as type rules."],["compilation","What does code generation produce?",["Target instructions or intermediate code","An external USB drive","A process time quantum","Printer toner"],0,"Code generation emits target representation."],["compilation","What does constant folding do?",["Evaluates a constant expression during compilation when safe","Encrypts the source","Randomly removes tokens","Creates source comments"],0,"A safe constant expression can be replaced by its value."],["compilation","Which is an optimisation risk/trade-off?",["Increased compilation time or debugging complexity","All compiled code becomes incorrect","Guaranteed no errors","No executable can be generated"],0,"Optimisation can make builds longer and debugging less direct."],["compilation","What does an AST represent?",["The structure of parsed source code","Free hard-drive space","The OS's ready queue","A BIOS signature"],0,"AST captures programme syntax at a structural level."],["linking","What resolves references between separately compiled modules?",["Linker","Hardware clock","Keyboard driver","Page allocator"],0,"Linking connects object files and external symbols."],["linking","What does the loader do?",["Prepares the executable in memory for execution","Turns source words into tokens","Copies files to a DVD only","Plans a classroom lesson"],0,"The loader maps the programme, dependencies and initial state."],["linking","What is a shared/dynamic library?",["Code loaded or linked separately that programmes may reuse","An unmodifiable printed book","Only a USB flash chip","A hard-coded page table"],0,"Dynamic libraries can be shared and resolved later."],["linking","Why use a library?",["Reuse tested routines rather than rewrite them","Prevent all future security updates","Remove the need for a runtime","Guarantee zero licensing obligations"],0,"Libraries can save development time and improve consistency."],["linking","What is a potential drawback of dynamic linking?",["A missing/incompatible dependency may prevent execution","Every executable must contain all code","Libraries cannot be updated","It prevents file sharing"],0,"Runtime dependencies need compatible versions."],["linking","What does relocation refer to?",["Adjusting address references for the memory location actually used","Moving a keyboard on the desk","Deleting token whitespace","Replacing an ISR"],0,"Relocation makes addresses valid for the loaded programme."],["linking","Which sequence best describes a conventional native application build/run path?",["Compile → link → load → execute","Load → compile → delete → output","Interpret → printer → BIOS → link","Backup → schedule → tokenise → save"],0,"Separate translation, linking and loading responsibilities."]],"writing":[["1.2.1(a)","A regional archive is installing public terminals for finding and saving catalogue records. Explain three operating-system services that make the terminals usable and reliable.","A strong response develops three distinct functions: the user interface presents commands/results; memory management allocates and protects browser working memory; the scheduler balances browser and background tasks; file and device management provide secure save/print access."],["1.2.1(b)","A teaching machine has 512-byte pages. Virtual address 1,410 belongs to a page mapped to physical frame 9. Work out the page number, offset and physical address.","Page = floor(1410/512) = 2; offset = 386; physical address = 9×512+386 = 4,994. Explain why the offset is unchanged."],["1.2.1(b)","A sound-production programme pauses intermittently while loading a large sample set. Explain how page faults and virtual-memory backing storage may be involved.","If needed virtual pages are absent from RAM, page faults cause reads from slower backing storage. Frequent faults/swapping can delay execution; additional RAM may help when the active working set is too large."],["1.2.1(c)","During a theatre show, a controller receives an urgent stop signal while preparing a normal lighting cue. Describe a possible interrupt response sequence.","Recognise a high-priority request at an appropriate instruction boundary, preserve execution context, run the relevant ISR to enter a safe state, clear/acknowledge the interrupt, then restore or otherwise handle the prior task appropriately."],["1.2.1(d)","Three CPU-only tasks arrive together: Finch 4 cycles, Rowan 1 cycle, Elm 3 cycles. Show their FCFS completion times in that order, then compare with SJF.","FCFS finish at 4, 5, 8. SJF order Rowan→Elm→Finch; finish at 1, 4, 8. Explain shorter average waiting for this workload but not universal suitability."],["1.2.1(d)","A music playback application must respond to controls quickly while background exports are CPU intensive. Compare RR and FCFS scheduling for this situation.","RR time sharing prevents a long export monopolising one core and can improve responsiveness. FCFS may make the control process wait behind the export, though RR adds context-switch overhead and scheduling choices affect responsiveness."],["1.2.1(e)","A coastal warning device has a strict deadline after a water-level threshold is reached. Justify an OS type and explain a relevant design trade-off.","An embedded real-time OS supports a specific hardware function and predictable deadline response. Reliability, prioritisation and bounded response matter more than broad application compatibility."],["1.2.1(f)","A newly assembled PC displays firmware diagnostics before an OS appears. Explain the main steps from power-on to user login.","Non-volatile BIOS/UEFI initialises and checks essential hardware, identifies a bootable device, starts a boot loader, loads the OS kernel into memory and transfers control to it; the OS initialises services and login."],["1.2.1(g)","A new label printer is connected to a laboratory computer but cannot print correctly without suitable software. Explain the role of a driver.","The driver provides the OS with device-specific commands and interfaces, handles data transfer/status and converts general print requests into the printer's expected control protocol. Compatibility with hardware and OS matters."],["1.2.1(h)","A company needs a test environment for a second operating system on its existing desktops. Explain two benefits and two limitations of VMs.","Benefits include separate guest OS testing and flexible use of physical hardware. Limitations include overhead, management/compatibility requirements and security configuration; isolation is helpful but not absolute."],["1.2.2(a)","A local theatre needs a booking application for 200 seats, online payments and accessible seating rules. Explain what criteria should drive a bespoke versus off-the-shelf decision.","Consider required functionality and integrations, accessibility, security, cost, licence and maintenance. Existing software enables quick deployment, while bespoke could better implement unusual seating rules at higher development cost."],["1.2.2(b)","A research team shares many unique sound recordings. Recommend two utilities and explain how each supports reliable ongoing work.","Versioned automatic backup with tested recovery protects recordings; anti-malware protects hosts; compression can reduce transfer/storage; updates patch vulnerabilities. Develop two clear recommendations tied to the workflow."],["1.2.2(c)","An environmental charity wants to adapt an existing data-collection application to support an uncommon sensor. Compare open-source and closed-source choices.","Appropriately licensed open source may allow inspection and modification; closed source may provide integrated vendor support but restrict code changes. Check hardware compatibility, licences, support, maintenance and cost."],["1.2.2(d)","Explain why an intermediate language such as bytecode can make a programme easier to distribute to different computer platforms.","A compiler can target a shared intermediate instruction set; a compatible VM/runtime on each platform interprets or compiles the bytecode. The runtime is platform-specific; the programme can often be reused unchanged."],["1.2.2(d)","A programme is used repeatedly on several hundred machines of one supported architecture. Explain when ahead-of-time compilation might be appropriate and mention a limitation.","A native executable can be distributed without translating source on each run and can be optimised. It may be platform-specific and require new builds for other architectures; tools still need testing."],["1.2.2(e)","For the fresh source statement `price = 6 * units`, outline lexical analysis, parsing and semantic checking without assuming any given values for `units`.","Lexing emits identifiers, assignment, integer and operator tokens. Parsing checks grammar and constructs expression structure. Semantic checks confirm `units` is a valid identifier and compatible type; the supplied statement alone does not establish whether it was previously declared."],["1.2.2(e)","A compiler safely replaces `8 * 5` with the constant 40. Name the technique and explain a benefit and possible limitation.","Constant folding is a code optimisation that reduces runtime arithmetic. It must preserve semantics and may make optimised code harder to trace back to source during debugging."],["1.2.2(f)","Two independently developed modules use a third-party encryption library. Explain how the linker and loader help the application start.","The linker combines compiled modules, resolves external references and links/imports required library routines. The loader maps executable code/dependencies into memory, performs required relocations and starts execution."],["1.2.2(f)","A team is deciding between static and dynamic library linking for a small offline mapping programme. Give one advantage and drawback of each.","Static linking can package required library code into a self-contained executable but increases size and may require rebuilding for library updates. Dynamic linking can share/update libraries separately but depends on compatible versions being available."]],"resources":{"craig4":"https://craigndave.org/video-categories/slr-4-operating-systems-systems-software/","craig5":"https://craigndave.org/video-categories/slr-5-application-generation/","isaac":"https://isaaccomputerscience.org/topics/a_level?examBoard=ocr&stage=a_level#ocr","pmt":"https://www.physicsandmathstutor.com/computer-science-revision/a-level-ocr/software-and-software-development/","wizard4":"https://compsciwizard.com/topics/1.2.1","wizard5":"https://compsciwizard.com/topics/1.2.2","ocr":"https://www.ocr.org.uk/Images/170844-specification-accredited-a-level-gce-computer-science-h446.pdf"}};
